--TEST--
A written `Core\Cap::has` naming something outside the roster is refused while compiling, because `false` is what a merely ungranted capability answers too — ADR 0112 §§ 6, 8
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0112 § 8's roster is closed, and the two spellings below are the two
// ways a misspelling arrives: a capability that does not exist, and a name
// spelled the way a neighbouring system spells it. Both would answer `false`
// on every deployment, which is exactly what a real capability nobody granted
// answers, so the branch would simply never run and nothing at run time could
// say why.
if (Core\Cap::has("fs.delete")) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
}

if (Core\Cap::has("network.connect")) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
}

// The bound on the other side: the real name compiles, in the same file, so
// what is being refused is the name and not the member.
if (Core\Cap::has("net.connect")) {
    echo "net.connect: granted\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0616]: `fs.delete` is not a capability
%A
error[E0616]: `network.connect` is not a capability
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
